{"metadata":{"externalID":"FunctionalGraphs","modules":[{"name":"FunctionalGraphs"}],"role":"collection","roleHeading":"Framework","symbolKind":"module","title":"FunctionalGraphs"},"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"topicSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Structures","title":"Structures"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/brentCycleDetection(from:successor:)","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/floydCycleDetection(from:successor:)"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Functions","title":"Functions"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[[]]},"kind":"symbol","references":{"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","title":"FunctionalGraphs","role":"collection","abstract":[],"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","kind":"symbol"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph","title":"FunctionalGraph","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices "},{"code":"0..<n","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":": every vertex "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" has exactly one out-edge,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"v → f(v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph (Laaksonen,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Competitive Programmer’s Handbook","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":", §16.3)."}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Function